Revision history for Test::Consumer::Contract

0.02	2026-10-16
	- Paths are no longer taken from the platform when the workspace is scanned.
	  File::Spec->abs2rel() answers with '\' on Windows while every registry key,
	  ignored rule and failure message names files with '/', so the discovered
	  path matched no consumer in the declaration and every consumer was reported
	  as unregistered - check 3 failed on Windows for a contract that holds.  The
	  paths the scan reports are now spelled the way the declaration spells them,
	  on every platform.  The directories the scan walks past were matched with
	  '/' alone, so on Windows they were not walked past at all and the files
	  scanned were the platform's choice rather than the contract's; the name is
	  now matched at a boundary of either separator.  t/scanner.t holds the
	  spellings to each other and t/contract.t runs the three checks against a
	  workspace whose paths use '\', so neither failure can come back on a platform
	  the suite is not run on.
	- t/changes-version.t, an author test that the version in the newest Changes
	  entry is the version every module under lib/ declares.  A release is two
	  edits - the $VERSION and a heading - and the heading is the one that gets
	  forgotten, so this fails with both numbers named instead of letting a
	  version be uploaded with no note to say what changed in it.  It runs with
	  AUTHOR_TESTING=1, as the other author tests do, and each module is loaded
	  and asked for its $VERSION rather than read for one, so a commented-out
	  assignment cannot pass for a declaration.
	- The end-to-end tests run their fixtures without a shell, so the suite passes
	  on Windows.  t/contract.t built one command string with POSIX quoting and a
	  "2>&1" on the end, and a single-string open is run by the platform's shell:
	  cmd.exe does not know what a POSIX single quote is, so the whole command
	  became one invalid file name and every subtest read "The filename, directory
	  name, or volume label syntax is incorrect." instead of the check it was
	  asserting on.  The interpreter, the include path and the fixture test are
	  now passed as separate arguments, and the fixture test puts everything the
	  runner has to read on one stream itself - Test::Builder's own diagnostic
	  stream is pointed at stdout and STDERR is merged into it - which is what the
	  shell's redirection used to do for it.
	- The accessor check now runs in both directions.  It already required every
	  documented accessor the contract names to exist on the value object; it now
	  also requires every accessor the value object documents - its =head2 (and
	  deeper) headings and its public subs - to be accounted for in 'accessors',
	  'not_accessors' or 'conditional', failing with the name and the three places
	  it could belong.  A value object that gains a documented accessor therefore
	  fails the contract until the declaration says what kind of accessor it is,
	  instead of the declaration being left behind - and a consumer that reads the
	  new accessor passing check 2 under a contract that never mentioned it.
	- New option 'not_accessors': documented accessors of the value object that
	  are not a slot of one result (a constructor, a class constant), as
	  name => why.  An entry the value object does not document fails, as an entry
	  in 'accessors' does.
	- New method documented_accessors(), the names the value object documents, so
	  a declaration can be checked from outside the module.
	- t/contract.t gains the scenarios that prove the new direction bites: a value
	  object that gains a documented accessor (implemented, and documented only,
	  through AUTOLOAD), the same accessor accounted for in 'not_accessors' and in
	  'conditional', and a name the value object no longer documents.

0.01	2026-10-15
	- First version, lifted from the reader/producer contract that lived inside
	  Geo-Coder-Free/t/consumer-contract.t, so any distribution in the workspace
	  can declare its producer keys and registered consumers and get the same
	  three checks.
